# M26 — posture rule E3 (the executable set equals the registry) made unmatchable.
#
# Before 20261022160000 the executable surface of schema `app` was SIX functions, not the
# three entry points: `current_user_id()`, `require_user_id()` and
# `derive_decision_state(...)` are granted to `authenticated` by 20261021140100 lines
# 147-149. Those three are deliberate and disclose nothing — two return the caller's own
# identity, the third is IMMUTABLE and reads no table — but the number was discovered by
# querying the live catalogue, because nothing asserted it anywhere.
#
# With the schema exposed, every one of those grants is an HTTP endpoint and a seventh
# added carelessly becomes one silently. Rule E3 is what turns "we think there are six"
# into a committed list with a reason per row. This mutation makes the unregistered-function
# branch unreachable, which is what a reviewer "simplifying" the predicate would do by
# accident.
id: M26
title: posture rule E3 (app functions executable by a client role must be registered) made unmatchable
expect: caught
proves: rule E3 reports a new app function granted to a client role without a reviewed registry entry
expect-test: [NFR-02] E3 fires on an app function granted to a client role but unregistered

file: supabase/migrations/20261022160000_expose_app_schema_to_postgrest.sql
count: 1
@@FIND
       and not exists (select 1 from public.posture_rest_allow a where a.signature =
@@REPLACE
       and false and not exists (select 1 from public.posture_rest_allow a where a.signature =
@@END
